@.str.s8046 = private unnamed_addr constant [3092 x i8] c"\0A    Usage:\0A\0A    Project commands -- these read build.ums, found at or above the\0A    working directory, and write to .prismio/build/<profile>/:\0A      prismio init [name]                        start a project here, or in a new directory\0A      prismio build [--release] [target...]      build every target, or the ones named\0A      prismio run [--release] [target] [-- args...]\0A                                                 build, then run the executable target\0A      prismio test [--release] [test...]         build and run the test targets\0A      prismio clean [--release]                  remove this profile's build output\0A      prismio <name> [args...]                   run a command the manifest declares\0A\0A    Single-file commands -- these name a source and need no manifest:\0A      prismio build <source.psm> [-o output] [-g] [-O0..-O3|-Os|-Oz] [--overflow-checks]\0A                    [--target <triple>] [--sysroot <sdk>]\0A                    [--freestanding [--native <file>]... [--native-flag <flag>]...\0A                     [--link-arg <arg>]...\0A                     [--target-features <list>] [--code-model <model>] [--no-red-zone]]\0A      prismio run <source.psm> [-o output] [-g] [--overflow-checks] [--jit]\0A                    [-- args...]\0A      prismio check <source.psm> [--diagnostic-format=json]\0A                    [--overlay <file.psm> <text>] [--module <name>]\0A\0A      prismio --version            which compiler, LLVM and standard library\0A      prismio --help-all           every command and flag, the compiler-development ones too\0A\0A          The debug profile builds with -g and --overflow-checks; --release\0A          builds optimised with neither. A manifest can change either in a\0A          `profiles` block.\0A\0A          -g emits DWARF, so the program can be run under lldb or gdb, and\0A          drops the object step to -O0: a variable folded into a register is a\0A          variable a debugger cannot show you.\0A\0A          -O0..-O3 set how much the program is optimised: -O3 without -g, -O0\0A          with it, when none is given. -Os and -Oz optimise for size; -Oz also\0A          outlines repeated machine code, smaller and usually slower.\0A\0A          --freestanding builds for a machine with no operating system: the\0A          installed runtime is not linked, no C library or startup files are,\0A          and the image is static. `main` is the function your entry stub calls.\0A          --native adds a C or assembly source the toolchain's clang compiles\0A          and links (the boot stub), --native-flag passes one flag to that\0A          compile (-mgeneral-regs-only), --link-arg passes one argument to the\0A          linker (-T kernel.ld), and --target-features, --code-model and\0A          --no-red-zone set what a kernel must (see docs/FREESTANDING_PLAN.md).\0A          The program provides every runtime symbol it uses.\0A\0A          --target takes an LLVM target triple -- x86_64-apple-macos,\0A          aarch64-linux-android, x86_64-pc-windows-msvc,\0A          x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, wasm32-unknown-unknown. --sysroot points\0A          clang at that target's SDK.\0A    \00"
@USAGE = internal global %prismio.str { ptr @.str.s8046, i64 17179872275 }
@.str.s8047 = private unnamed_addr constant [1101 x i8] c"\0A    Compiler-development commands:\0A      prismio runtime-hash\0A      prismio dump-ast <source.psm>\0A      prismio aif <source.psm> [--manifest] [--summary] [--layout] [--copyable-collections]\0A                               [--theta-fields] [--budget=N] [--target <triple>]\0A      prismio aif <source.psm> --why=<ID|symbol>   explain one allocation decision\0A      prismio build|aif <source.psm> --force-layout=<Type>:<hot>   emit a named layout candidate\0A      prismio build <source.psm> --verify | --debug\0A\0A          --debug is the zero-analysis level and says nothing about debug info;\0A          --verify builds with the allocation ledger.\0A\0A          check --overlay reads <file.psm>, wherever the program reaches it, from\0A          the file <text> instead -- an editor's unsaved buffer. Check a module\0A          through its program's entry, since a module uses names its program\0A          shares: `prismio check src/main.psm --overlay src/parse/stmt.psm buf`.\0A          --module names a module checked on its own, as its importers name it:\0A          `prismio check std/map.psm --module std.map`.\0A    \00"
